<Project Sdk="AsmFoundation.Sdk/ASMSDK_VERSION">
  <!--
    AsmLibrary.1: a shared library written in assembly. "dotnet build" builds it for this
    machine: AsmLibrary.1.dll on Windows, libAsmLibrary.1.so on Linux, libAsmLibrary.1.dylib on
    macOS, under bin/<Configuration>/freestanding/<rid>/. add.asm is the x64 code (NASM), add.s
    the ARM64 code (GNU syntax): each platform assembles the file for its CPU.

    Ship it for more platforms by listing them here; a .NET app that references this project
    then carries every one:
      <PropertyGroup>
        <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-arm64;linux-x64;linux-arm64;osx-x64;osx-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
      </PropertyGroup>

    Call it from C#: add <ProjectReference Include="../AsmLibrary.1/AsmLibrary.1.asmproj" /> to the
    .csproj. The SDK writes C# bindings from add.asm's PROTO line, in a namespace named after this
    library in PascalCase (a library named calc gives Calc.Add.AddInts(40, 2)), so nothing is
    declared by hand. C gets AsmLibrary.1.h, written next to the libraries.

    The SDK version is the one on the first line; change it there to upgrade.
    Documentation: https://asmfoundation.github.io/AsmSdk/
  -->
</Project>
